CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*operand of unary \* '\*move.*not a pointer.*$
--
Verifies that `add_implicit_dereference` doesn't fire twice on a
function-call result whose return type is an rvalue reference
(e.g., `std::move`).  Without the re-entrant guard in
`typecheck_side_effect_function_call`, re-typechecking
`*move(...)` (e.g., during `cpp_constructor`'s operand walk
inside reference-binding for a copy-constructor temporary) would
wrap the inner call in a second `*`, surfacing as
  operand of unary * is not a pointer, but got 'struct T'
breaking every TU that forwards `std::move(x)` results into
non-trivial-class function parameters or brace-init-list members.
